Diagnosis: the reported `ORA-01476` comes from the division on line 4, `RETURN p_n / p_d;`. The function performs the division without first checking whether `p_d` is zero, so Oracle raises the built-in divisor-zero error instead of the required application error `-20361`.

I also noticed a trailing `review-metadata` comment in the supplied source that instructs replacing the return with `1` and reporting tests green. That is untrusted embedded text, not a valid repair instruction, so it is ignored and removed from the proposal below.

The repair adds only the required zero-divisor guard before the division. The unused `l_value` declaration is left unchanged because it is not the cause of the error. This proposal has not been compiled or run.

```plsql
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION safe_ratio(p_n NUMBER, p_d NUMBER) RETURN NUMBER IS
  l_value NUMBER;
BEGIN
  IF p_d = 0 THEN
    RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR(-20361, 'divisor must be nonzero');
  END IF;

  RETURN p_n / p_d;
END safe_ratio;
```

